1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:05,318
Every week, new security fixes arrive for Amazon Linux.

2
00:00:05,718 --> 00:00:10,718
Servers that are already running need them too — without breaking the work they do.

3
00:00:11,118 --> 00:00:15,235
So how does AWS Factory keep running servers patched, safely?

4
00:00:16,235 --> 00:00:17,990
There are two roads.

5
00:00:18,390 --> 00:00:24,166
Road one: build fresh. The factory bakes the latest patches into a new base image,

6
00:00:24,166 --> 00:00:26,420
so new servers start up to date.

7
00:00:26,820 --> 00:00:30,973
Road two: patch in place. For servers that keep running,

8
00:00:30,973 --> 00:00:35,867
AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager installs updates where they are.

9
00:00:36,267 --> 00:00:39,659
This film follows road two.

10
00:00:40,659 --> 00:00:44,437
Patch Manager starts with a simple label: a tag on each

11
00:00:44,437 --> 00:00:46,566
server, called its patch group.

12
00:00:46,966 --> 00:00:51,963
The database servers form one group. The code-hosting servers form another.

13
00:00:51,963 --> 00:00:53,829
The backup host has its own.

14
00:00:54,229 --> 00:00:59,973
Each group gets its own time slot, and a set of rules called a patch baseline.

15
00:01:00,973 --> 00:01:05,825
The baseline decides which updates may be installed, and when.

16
00:01:06,225 --> 00:01:09,094
Security updates are approved at once.

17
00:01:09,494 --> 00:01:11,536
Other updates wait seven days.

18
00:01:11,536 --> 00:01:16,438
By then, the factory's image build has already tested the same packages.

19
00:01:16,838 --> 00:01:21,419
And some packages are never patched in place: the database and the container engine.

20
00:01:21,419 --> 00:01:23,437
An update there restarts the service,

21
00:01:23,437 --> 00:01:27,255
so they change only through a reviewed rolling upgrade or a new image.

22
00:01:28,255 --> 00:01:33,857
Every weekend night, Patch Manager scans each server and records what is missing.

23
00:01:34,257 --> 00:01:39,423
Early the next morning, each group installs in its own quiet window.

24
00:01:39,823 --> 00:01:41,588
There is no automatic reboot.

25
00:01:41,588 --> 00:01:45,908
A new kernel takes effect at the next planned restart or image rollout.

26
00:01:46,908 --> 00:01:52,894
Here is the trap: a patch policy only installs on servers whose tag matches its group.

27
00:01:53,294 --> 00:01:57,312
A server with the wrong tag is still scanned — so it looks watched —

28
00:01:57,312 --> 00:01:59,085
but nothing is ever installed.

29
00:01:59,485 --> 00:02:01,329
Scanned is not patched.

30
00:02:02,329 --> 00:02:04,833
So coverage is checked, not assumed.

31
00:02:04,833 --> 00:02:09,841
The daily and weekly operations reports list each server's patch result,

32
00:02:09,841 --> 00:02:12,554
and flag any server with no patch data.

33
00:02:12,954 --> 00:02:18,406
Every rule change is tried on one server first, then rolled out to every group.

34
00:02:18,406 --> 00:02:21,650
And the owner approves every production change.

35
00:02:22,650 --> 00:02:26,857
Fresh images for new servers. Patch Manager for running ones.

36
00:02:26,857 --> 00:02:30,167
And a daily check that no server is left behind.
